1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:04,937
Picture it. A bucket of zinnias on the back
of your truck. Fifty stems, cut at dawn.

2
00:00:05,217 --> 00:00:07,897
Sold to a florist for
about a dollar a stem.

3
00:00:08,183 --> 00:00:10,839
Now picture the same
zinnias in a mason jar,

4
00:00:10,859 --> 00:00:12,524
on a kitchen table in town.

5
00:00:12,544 --> 00:00:16,912
Arranged by a woman who paid fifty-five
dollars to pick them herself.

6
00:00:17,183 --> 00:00:18,906
Same flowers. Same field.

7
00:00:18,926 --> 00:00:20,373
A very different check.

8
00:00:20,650 --> 00:00:23,911
That's the power of a flower
arranging workshop on your farm.

9
00:00:24,183 --> 00:00:28,913
Craft classes were the fastest-growing
kind of experience on Tripadvisor in the

10
00:00:28,933 --> 00:00:30,837
first half of twenty twenty-six.

11
00:00:30,857 --> 00:00:33,845
Bookings were up eighty-eight
percent on the year before.

12
00:00:34,117 --> 00:00:36,137
People want to make
something with their hands.

13
00:00:36,157 --> 00:00:38,562
And you have the field
they want to make it in.

14
00:00:38,850 --> 00:00:40,373
You don't need a florist's degree.

15
00:00:40,393 --> 00:00:42,062
You don't need a fancy venue.

16
00:00:42,082 --> 00:00:43,561
You don't need a big farm.

17
00:00:43,850 --> 00:00:44,841
Maybe you're thinking,

18
00:00:44,861 --> 00:00:48,224
"Who would pay to cut my flowers?
I'm a grower, not a teacher."

19
00:00:49,317 --> 00:00:51,069
Stay with me. In this video,

20
00:00:51,089 --> 00:00:55,855
we will build a flower arranging workshop
on your farm, step by step. Pick, arrange,

21
00:00:55,875 --> 00:00:57,455
take home, in five steps.

22
00:00:57,717 --> 00:01:01,526
And number four is the one most
farmers get wrong, so stay for it.

23
00:01:01,817 --> 00:01:03,759
I'm Sarah. Yes, I'm a cartoon.

24
00:01:03,779 --> 00:01:05,626
This channel comes from Stephen Loke,

25
00:01:05,646 --> 00:01:09,742
a durian farmer in Malaysia who
welcomes visitors to his farm.

26
00:01:10,017 --> 00:01:11,673
And here's what visitors taught him.

27
00:01:11,693 --> 00:01:13,127
They don't come for the crop.

28
00:01:13,147 --> 00:01:14,513
They come for the morning.

29
00:01:15,683 --> 00:01:18,666
Let me tell you about a young
mother in Washington State.

30
00:01:18,686 --> 00:01:20,649
Her name is Erin Benzakein.

31
00:01:20,917 --> 00:01:23,106
Her great-grandmother loved sweet peas.

32
00:01:23,126 --> 00:01:24,342
When she passed away,

33
00:01:24,362 --> 00:01:26,964
Erin planted a patch
of them in her memory.

34
00:01:27,250 --> 00:01:30,918
But a patch of sweet peas doesn't
stay a patch. They grew, and grew.

35
00:01:30,938 --> 00:01:33,389
She had more flowers
than she could ever use.

36
00:01:33,650 --> 00:01:36,391
So she gave them away,
to neighbors, to friends,

37
00:01:36,411 --> 00:01:37,942
to anyone who came by.

38
00:01:38,217 --> 00:01:42,090
Then someone asked to buy a jar of
flowers for a friend. Then another.

39
00:01:42,383 --> 00:01:43,758
That was the start of Floret,

40
00:01:43,778 --> 00:01:46,490
a family flower farm in the Skagit Valley.

41
00:01:46,783 --> 00:01:47,815
But here's the turn.

42
00:01:47,835 --> 00:01:49,899
People didn't only want her flowers.

43
00:01:49,919 --> 00:01:51,724
They wanted to know how she did it.

44
00:01:52,817 --> 00:01:55,014
So she opened her gate and taught a class.

45
00:01:55,034 --> 00:01:58,867
Tickets for the first workshop
sold out within a couple of hours.

46
00:01:59,150 --> 00:02:00,222
To meet the demand,

47
00:02:00,242 --> 00:02:02,017
they added two more sessions.

48
00:02:02,283 --> 00:02:05,580
Floret says those first workshops
were rough around the edges.

49
00:02:05,600 --> 00:02:06,644
After each one,

50
00:02:06,664 --> 00:02:10,335
the team spent a week talking
over what to fix. They got better.

51
00:02:10,355 --> 00:02:13,342
Today those lessons live
on as online courses.

52
00:02:13,617 --> 00:02:17,386
Erin's classes were for people who
wanted to grow flowers for a living.

53
00:02:17,406 --> 00:02:19,479
Yours will be simpler. A family.

54
00:02:19,499 --> 00:02:21,719
A group of friends. A birthday.

55
00:02:21,983 --> 00:02:23,459
But the lesson is the same.

56
00:02:23,479 --> 00:02:26,141
People will pay to stand
in your field and learn.

57
00:02:27,317 --> 00:02:29,747
Stephen calls it the
Bucket-to-Bouquet Flip.

58
00:02:30,017 --> 00:02:32,195
The old way: you cut, you bucket,

59
00:02:32,215 --> 00:02:33,374
you sell by the stem.

60
00:02:33,394 --> 00:02:34,739
The buyer sets the price,

61
00:02:34,759 --> 00:02:36,993
and every stem has to be perfect.

62
00:02:37,283 --> 00:02:38,817
The new way: the guest cuts,

63
00:02:38,837 --> 00:02:39,977
the guest arranges,

64
00:02:39,997 --> 00:02:41,299
the guest carries it home.

65
00:02:41,319 --> 00:02:42,354
You set the price,

66
00:02:42,374 --> 00:02:44,918
and you're paid for the
morning, not the stem.

67
00:02:45,183 --> 00:02:46,008
Do the sums.

68
00:02:46,028 --> 00:02:51,100
One workshop of ten guests at fifty-five
dollars is five hundred and fifty dollars.

69
00:02:51,383 --> 00:02:53,054
To take that in by the bucket,

70
00:02:53,074 --> 00:02:54,499
at about a dollar a stem,

71
00:02:54,519 --> 00:02:57,998
you'd have to cut and sell
five hundred and fifty stems.

72
00:02:58,283 --> 00:02:59,753
Now, what this is not.

73
00:02:59,773 --> 00:03:03,867
Stephen isn't telling you to stop
selling to florists. Keep your buckets.

74
00:03:04,150 --> 00:03:06,915
A workshop is one more way
to sell the same field,

75
00:03:06,935 --> 00:03:08,374
on the days you choose.

76
00:03:08,650 --> 00:03:10,539
If you want more ideas like this,

77
00:03:10,559 --> 00:03:12,245
Stephen created a free ebook,

78
00:03:12,265 --> 00:03:16,352
One Hundred and Fifty Ways to
Unlock Hidden Wealth From Your Farm.

79
00:03:16,372 --> 00:03:18,316
The link is in the description.

80
00:03:18,583 --> 00:03:20,711
So how do you start? Five steps.

81
00:03:21,883 --> 00:03:23,664
Number one. Plant for the workshop,

82
00:03:23,684 --> 00:03:25,075
not just the market.

83
00:03:25,350 --> 00:03:27,227
Workshop flowers need to be tough,

84
00:03:27,247 --> 00:03:28,608
cheerful and plentiful.

85
00:03:28,628 --> 00:03:31,144
Cut-and-come-again
flowers are your friends.

86
00:03:31,417 --> 00:03:33,748
Zinnias, cosmos, snapdragons,

87
00:03:33,768 --> 00:03:37,823
branching sunflowers and dahlias. The
more you cut, the more they bloom.

88
00:03:38,117 --> 00:03:40,055
Add greenery. Mint, basil,

89
00:03:40,075 --> 00:03:41,104
herbs from the garden,

90
00:03:41,124 --> 00:03:42,651
grasses from the hedgerow.

91
00:03:42,917 --> 00:03:44,476
Greens make a jar look full,

92
00:03:44,496 --> 00:03:46,421
and they cost you almost nothing.

93
00:03:46,683 --> 00:03:47,921
And here's a secret.

94
00:03:47,941 --> 00:03:49,605
The stems florists turn down,

95
00:03:49,625 --> 00:03:51,486
the short ones and the bent ones,

96
00:03:51,506 --> 00:03:52,947
are perfect in a jar.

97
00:03:52,967 --> 00:03:55,218
Your seconds become somebody's favorite.

98
00:03:56,383 --> 00:03:58,253
Number two. Set the table.

99
00:03:58,517 --> 00:04:02,563
You don't need a venue. A barn, a
shed, a shady tree. A long table,

100
00:04:02,583 --> 00:04:04,305
and one station per guest.

101
00:04:04,583 --> 00:04:05,684
At each station,

102
00:04:05,704 --> 00:04:06,869
a jar or a vase,

103
00:04:06,889 --> 00:04:08,045
a bucket of water,

104
00:04:08,065 --> 00:04:09,766
a pair of snips and an apron.

105
00:04:09,786 --> 00:04:12,654
Put a little card by each
jar with the guest's name.

106
00:04:12,917 --> 00:04:16,297
Keep one big bucket of extras
in the middle, cut that morning,

107
00:04:16,317 --> 00:04:17,933
so nobody runs short.

108
00:04:18,217 --> 00:04:19,316
Keep the group small.

109
00:04:19,336 --> 00:04:22,213
Eight to twelve guests is
plenty for one teacher.

110
00:04:22,483 --> 00:04:23,954
Everyone gets your attention,

111
00:04:23,974 --> 00:04:26,396
and everyone goes home
with something lovely.

112
00:04:27,583 --> 00:04:31,235
Number three. Run it in three
parts. Pick, arrange, take home.

113
00:04:31,517 --> 00:04:34,704
Pick. Give each guest a bucket
and walk them through the rows.

114
00:04:34,983 --> 00:04:36,151
Show them where to cut,

115
00:04:36,171 --> 00:04:37,674
just above a pair of leaves,

116
00:04:37,694 --> 00:04:40,102
so the plant branches and blooms again.

117
00:04:40,383 --> 00:04:42,301
Twenty to thirty minutes is enough.

118
00:04:42,321 --> 00:04:44,430
This is the part they'll photograph.

119
00:04:44,717 --> 00:04:47,126
Arrange. Teach one simple recipe.

120
00:04:47,417 --> 00:04:49,093
Greens first, to make a frame.

121
00:04:49,113 --> 00:04:51,161
Then three big blooms, the stars.

122
00:04:51,181 --> 00:04:53,426
Then fillers around the stars. Last,

123
00:04:53,446 --> 00:04:56,116
something light and
airy that dances on top.

124
00:04:56,383 --> 00:04:58,824
Greens, stars, fillers, dancers.

125
00:04:58,844 --> 00:05:00,690
Anyone can do it the first time.

126
00:05:00,983 --> 00:05:02,734
Take home. Wrap each jar.

127
00:05:02,754 --> 00:05:04,343
Hand over a small care card.

128
00:05:04,363 --> 00:05:05,708
Fresh water every day,

129
00:05:05,728 --> 00:05:07,116
and trim the stems.

130
00:05:07,383 --> 00:05:10,095
And take a photo of every
guest with their flowers.

131
00:05:10,115 --> 00:05:12,328
That photo will sell your next class.

132
00:05:13,517 --> 00:05:16,688
Number four. This is the
one most farmers get wrong.

133
00:05:16,950 --> 00:05:19,201
They price the flowers. Price the morning.

134
00:05:19,483 --> 00:05:21,299
Look at what real farms charge.

135
00:05:21,319 --> 00:05:23,209
Big Jim Farms in New Mexico,

136
00:05:23,229 --> 00:05:24,529
thirty-five dollars,

137
00:05:24,549 --> 00:05:26,972
with flowers and supplies included.

138
00:05:27,250 --> 00:05:28,920
Moulton Farm in New Hampshire,

139
00:05:28,940 --> 00:05:31,242
forty-nine dollars and ninety-nine cents,

140
00:05:31,262 --> 00:05:35,131
with a pound and a half of flowers
from their pick-your-own garden.

141
00:05:35,417 --> 00:05:37,398
Land's Sake Farm in Massachusetts,

142
00:05:37,418 --> 00:05:38,645
fifty-five dollars,

143
00:05:38,665 --> 00:05:40,599
flowers and jars included.

144
00:05:40,883 --> 00:05:44,802
And in England, a dahlia bouquet
class in Ledbury, fifty pounds.

145
00:05:45,083 --> 00:05:46,116
Notice something.

146
00:05:46,136 --> 00:05:47,967
Every one of them sells one price,

147
00:05:47,987 --> 00:05:49,169
with everything in.

148
00:05:49,189 --> 00:05:50,381
No counting stems.

149
00:05:50,401 --> 00:05:52,371
No extra charge for the jar.

150
00:05:52,650 --> 00:05:54,353
And sell tickets before the day.

151
00:05:54,373 --> 00:05:56,749
Paid in advance means people turn up.

152
00:05:57,917 --> 00:05:59,751
Number five. Fill the seats.

153
00:06:00,017 --> 00:06:02,335
List each date on a simple ticket page.

154
00:06:02,355 --> 00:06:05,817
Flower farms use sites like
Ticket Tailor and Eventbrite.

155
00:06:06,083 --> 00:06:10,095
Ask your local tourism office and
chamber of commerce to list it too.

156
00:06:10,115 --> 00:06:14,378
Moulton Farm's workshop is on its
regional chamber's events page.

157
00:06:14,650 --> 00:06:16,007
Give each class a season.

158
00:06:16,027 --> 00:06:17,266
Spring blossoms.

159
00:06:17,286 --> 00:06:18,337
Summer zinnias.

160
00:06:18,357 --> 00:06:20,308
A dahlia evening in September.

161
00:06:20,328 --> 00:06:22,313
A wreath class when the frost comes.

162
00:06:22,583 --> 00:06:25,520
Every season gives your
guests a reason to come back.

163
00:06:25,783 --> 00:06:27,867
And remember the photo from number three.

164
00:06:27,887 --> 00:06:31,870
Ask each guest if you can share it.
Their friends see it. Their friends book.

165
00:06:33,050 --> 00:06:35,180
But maybe you're thinking, "I'm a grower.

166
00:06:35,200 --> 00:06:37,383
I don't know how to arrange flowers."

167
00:06:38,450 --> 00:06:41,755
Good. Your guests don't want
a florist. They want a farmer.

168
00:06:42,017 --> 00:06:44,492
They want to know which
flower you planted first,

169
00:06:44,512 --> 00:06:46,379
and why the bees love the cosmos.

170
00:06:46,399 --> 00:06:49,109
You know your field
better than anyone alive.

171
00:06:49,383 --> 00:06:53,914
And the recipe from number three is all
the arranging you need. Greens, stars,

172
00:06:53,934 --> 00:06:55,368
fillers, dancers.

173
00:06:55,388 --> 00:06:59,030
Practice it ten times on your own
kitchen table, and you're ready.

174
00:06:59,317 --> 00:07:02,934
Too small? Erin Benzakein built
Floret on a two-acre farm.

175
00:07:02,954 --> 00:07:05,751
A few rows is enough
for one table of guests.

176
00:07:06,917 --> 00:07:09,632
Let's go back to that bucket
on the back of your truck.

177
00:07:09,652 --> 00:07:10,653
Fifty zinnias,

178
00:07:10,673 --> 00:07:12,296
at about a dollar a stem.

179
00:07:12,583 --> 00:07:14,528
Now picture ten people in your field,

180
00:07:14,548 --> 00:07:17,649
each with a bucket of their
own. Laughing. Snipping.

181
00:07:17,669 --> 00:07:20,583
Carrying a jar of your flowers
home to their kitchen table.

182
00:07:20,603 --> 00:07:22,510
That's the Bucket-to-Bouquet Flip.

183
00:07:23,583 --> 00:07:26,055
Watching won't change
anything. Doing will.

184
00:07:26,317 --> 00:07:27,466
So here's your homework.

185
00:07:27,486 --> 00:07:28,896
Walk your field this week.

186
00:07:28,916 --> 00:07:31,283
Which three flowers could
fill a jar for a guest?

187
00:07:31,303 --> 00:07:32,893
Tell Stephen in the comments.

188
00:07:32,913 --> 00:07:34,435
He reads every one.

189
00:07:34,717 --> 00:07:35,751
For the full plan,

190
00:07:35,771 --> 00:07:36,786
Stephen's ebook,

191
00:07:36,806 --> 00:07:38,820
How to Start a Flower Arranging Class,

192
00:07:38,840 --> 00:07:40,573
is linked in the description.

193
00:07:40,850 --> 00:07:42,431
And grab Stephen's free ebook,

194
00:07:42,451 --> 00:07:46,543
One Hundred and Fifty Ways to
Unlock Hidden Wealth From Your Farm.

195
00:07:46,817 --> 00:07:50,738
It's free because he wants you to
find the one idea that fits your farm.

196
00:07:51,017 --> 00:07:52,589
Click the link in the description,

197
00:07:52,609 --> 00:07:55,202
enter your name and email, and it's yours.

198
00:07:55,483 --> 00:07:57,709
Then pick the next video on your screen.

199
00:08:05,483 --> 00:08:07,670
Don't sell the stem. Sell the morning.

